Hi Jamal,

Thanks for including pandoc into the Ed Sharp editor!  I have been using Ed 
Sharp to edit Vinux documentation.  I then would switch to a terminal window 
in Vinux using VMware and run pandoc.  The directory where the files I am 
workinhg on are in a shared directory to make this easier.  I also count on 
bash history to repeatedly convert as I am making changes.  Ed Sharp will 
now make it even easier to create documentation with a single keystroke.

The announcement I am forwareding is a Windows user who has been tinkering 
with Vinux for Jaba cross development.   He has developed markup-vinder over 
the years to create user's manuals with markdown.  His utility creates a 
manual out of a directory tree of markdown text files.  It is now a Vinux 
package so that it is easily installed and updated.  It also installs 
pandoc, because it is listed as one of it's dependancies in the package.  I 
love that about Ubuntu!  I pass this on in case you are interested in 
expanding pandoc conversions to an entire directory to create complete 
manuals.

Don Marang

There is just so much stuff in the world that, to me, is devoid of any real 
substance, value, and content that I just try to make sure that I am working 
on things that matter.
Dean Kamen


